.TH SEED\-TO\-MASTER 1 2026-10-25 "blocktools 0.3.0" "blocktools manual"
.SH NAME
seed\-to\-master \- seed phrase (+ optional passphrase) \-> BIP32 master key(s)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
seed\-to\-master [\-\-passphrase\-prompt | \-\-passphrase\-file FILE] < {"mnemonic":...} lines \-> master keys (BIP39, Electrum standard/segwit, old Electrum 1.x 12\-word seed as scheme electrum\-old; 2FA: unsupported, planned)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
stdin JSON lines: optional {"passphrase":"..."} then {"mnemonic":"...","candidate":N}, or a bare phrase line.
A phrase is tried as BIP39, as an Electrum (2.x+) seed, and as an old Electrum 1.x seed
(12/24 words from the 1626\-word list, or the 32/64\-hex seed itself); every valid reading is
one output line with its "scheme". The old scheme has no BIP32 master: its line carries
"scheme":"electrum\-old" and the hex "seed", and `derive` turns it into m/{change}/{i} keys. If the phrase has extra words (e.g. a note
line), the trailing 12/13/15/18/21/24 words are tried too (sliding window).
\-\-passphrase\-prompt asks the human on /dev/tty with echo off (never argv/env).
\-\-passphrase\-file FILE: candidate passphrases, one per line (SwapChan SCAN_JS PASSPHRASES): each
.nf
.RS 2
  phrase is tried with the empty passphrase first, then every non\-empty line in file order; each
  output carries "passphrase_index" (0 = empty, N = line N of the non\-empty lines). The file's
  text is never printed. A stdin {"passphrase"} field is refused (bad_input) in this mode.
.RE
.fi
Output CONTAINS SECRETS ("xprv", "seed"). One job: phrase \-> master key.
.SH OPTIONS
.nf
  \-\-passphrase\-file FILE  one candidate passphrase per line; tried after the empty one; outputs carry passphrase_index
.fi
.SH INPUT/OUTPUT
stdin JSON lines: optional {"passphrase":"..."} then {"mnemonic":"...","candidate":N}, or a bare phrase line.
one output line with its "scheme". The old scheme has no BIP32 master: its line carries
.nf
.RS 2
  output carries "passphrase_index" (0 = empty, N = line N of the non\-empty lines). The file's
  text is never printed. A stdin {"passphrase"} field is refused (bad_input) in this mode.
.RE
.fi
Output CONTAINS SECRETS ("xprv", "seed"). One job: phrase \-> master key.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Measured on Linux by the EXAMPLES below (scripts/gen\-man.py runs them):
.TP
0
last status done
.TP
2
last status bad_input
.PP
Not every tool uses the same code for bad input: some exit 1, others exit 2 (docs/FINDINGS.md D1\-14). Always read the last JSON status line. Status and failure codes: docs/REFUSALS.md.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ echo "abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon about" | seed\-to\-master
# exit 0, last status: done
$ echo "powerful random nobody notice nothing important anyway look away hidden message over" | seed\-to\-master | derive \-\-chain btc \-\-count 1
# exit 0, last status: done
$ printf 'TREZOR\en' > pp.txt; echo "abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon about" | seed\-to\-master \-\-passphrase\-file pp.txt
# exit 0, last status: done
$ printf 'TREZOR\en' > pp.txt; seed\-to\-master \-\-passphrase\-file pp.txt \-\-passphrase\-prompt < /dev/null
# exit 2, last status: bad_input
.fi
.SH SECURITY NOTES
